<speak>
<p>Our three polynomials c, d, and g have product with their star equal to nine in the guiding case u equals three. At one and omega, their values lie on a circle of radius three. We want to show that all three share one rotation between these inputs. Our drawn points are an illustration, not values from an order thirty six circulant Hadamard matrix.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Recall delta of f: f of one divided by f of omega. The delta theorem makes all three deltas roots of unity of odd order. Compare d's phase change with c's by R equal to delta of d over delta of c. Define W similarly using g. These comparisons also have odd order. Here their candidates are one, omega, and omega squared.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The common signs give extra information: two divides d minus c, and one plus i divides g minus c, coefficient by coefficient. Both differences therefore vanish modulo one plus i. This is arithmetic, not a small angle.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Before taking ratios, check the denominators. At either input, one or omega, the inverse of c is its complex conjugate divided by nine. The same holds for d and g. We invert the odd number nine before reducing; its residue is one. Thus all these evaluations remain units.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Dividing by c now gives d over c and g over c each congruent to one at both inputs. R is the ratio of the two d over c values; W is the ratio of the two g over c values. Consequently R and W both have residue one.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Odd order and residue one now meet. If omega reduced to one, its vanishing sum, one plus omega plus omega squared, would become three ones. Two cancel in characteristic two, leaving one equal to zero. Omega squared is excluded too. In general, for a nontrivial root eta of odd order m, eta minus one times its geometric sum is zero. Cancel eta minus one before reduction. Reducing the sum gives an odd number of ones, again a contradiction. Therefore R and W equal one exactly.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Unpack this equality. The ratios d over c and g over c each keep their own value between inputs. They need not equal each other. In our schematic, these ratios are minus one and i. Set lambda equal to c of omega over c of one. The same lambda multiplies c, d, and g. Our illustrated lambda is omega: counterclockwise one hundred twenty degrees. Delta of c measures the reverse rotation.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Recall E of P: d of P minus c of P, divided by two, plus g of P minus c of P, divided by one plus i. This fixed linear combination inherits the common factor. Substitute lambda times each value at one, then factor lambda out. E of omega equals lambda E of one. The schematic has E of one equal to negative three plus three i, outside the circle.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Divide E of omega by c of omega. The common lambda cancels, leaving E of one over c of one. Both schematic normalized values are negative one plus i. This cancellation is an exact equality before modular reduction.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Separately, the hypothetical Hadamard construction requires E congruent to the nine term all ones polynomial. Its evaluations require E of one to have residue one and E of omega residue zero. These are requirements, not residues computed from our schematic arrows. Lambda remains invertible after reduction, so it cannot carry a nonzero residue to zero. Node seven writes the contradiction algebraically.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>For general odd u, the same rigidity gives R and W equal to one. With several distinct prime divisors, these compare alternating products. The two circle rotation describes our u equals three example; the deeper argument handles those products.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
